NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001987 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001988 "#pod =head1 SYNOPSIS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001989 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001990 "#pod   use Test::More tests => $Num_Tests;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001991 "#pod   use Test::Deep;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001992 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001993 "#pod   cmp_deeply("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001994 "#pod     $actual_horrible_nested_data_structure,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001995 "#pod     $expected_horrible_nested_data_structure,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001996 "#pod     \"got the right horrible nested data structure\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001997 "#pod   );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001998 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00001999 "#pod   cmp_deeply("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002000 "#pod     $object,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002001 "#pod     methods(name => \"John\", phone => \"55378008\"),"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002002 "#pod     \"object methods ok\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002003 "#pod   );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002004 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002005 "#pod   cmp_deeply("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002006 "#pod     \\@array,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002007 "#pod     [$hash1, $hash2, ignore()],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002008 "#pod     \"first 2 elements are as expected, ignoring 3\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002009 "#pod   );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002010 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002011 "#pod   cmp_deeply("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002012 "#pod     $object,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002013 "#pod     noclass({value => 5}),"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002014 "#pod     \"object looks ok, not checking its class\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002015 "#pod   );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002016 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002017 "#pod   cmp_deeply("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002018 "#pod     \\@result,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002019 "#pod     bag('a', 'b', {key => [1, 2]}),"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002020 "#pod     \"array has the 3 things we wanted in some order\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002021 "#pod   );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002022 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002023 "#pod =head1 DESCRIPTION"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002024 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002025 "#pod If you don't know anything about automated testing in Perl then you should"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002026 "#pod probably read about L<Test::Simple> and L<Test::More> before preceding."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002027 "#pod Test::Deep uses the L<Test::Builder> framework."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002028 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002029 "#pod Test::Deep gives you very flexible ways to check that the result you got is"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002030 "#pod the result you were expecting. At its simplest it compares two structures"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002031 "#pod by going through each level, ensuring that the values match, that arrays and"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002032 "#pod hashes have the same elements and that references are blessed into the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002033 "#pod correct class. It also handles circular data structures without getting"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002034 "#pod caught in an infinite loop."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002035 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002036 "#pod Where it becomes more interesting is in allowing you to do something besides"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002037 "#pod simple exact comparisons. With strings, the C<eq> operator checks that 2"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002038 "#pod strings are exactly equal but sometimes that's not what you want. When you"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002039 "#pod don't know exactly what the string should be but you do know some things"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002040 "#pod about how it should look, C<eq> is no good and you must use pattern matching"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002041 "#pod instead. Test::Deep provides pattern matching for complex data structures"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002042 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002043 "#pod Test::Deep has B<I<a lot>> of exports.  See L</EXPORTS> below."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002044 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002045 "#pod =head1 EXAMPLES"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002046 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002047 "#pod How Test::Deep works is much easier to understand by seeing some examples."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002048 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002049 "#pod =head2 Without Test::Deep"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002050 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002051 "#pod Say you want to test a function which returns a string. You know that your"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002052 "#pod string should be a 7 digit number beginning with 0, C<eq> is no good in this"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002053 "#pod situation, you need a regular expression. So you could use Test::More's"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002054 "#pod C<like()> function:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002055 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002056 "#pod   like($string, qr/^0[0-9]{6}$/, \"number looks good\");"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002057 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002058 "#pod Similarly, to check that a string looks like a name, you could do:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002059 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002060 "#pod   like($string, qr/^(Mr|Mrs|Miss) \\w+ \\w+$/,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002061 "#pod     \"got title, first and last name\");"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002062 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002063 "#pod Now imagine your function produces a hash with some personal details in it."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002064 "#pod You want to make sure that there are 2 keys, Name and Phone and that the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002065 "#pod name looks like a name and the phone number looks like a phone number. You"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002066 "#pod could do:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002067 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002068 "#pod   $hash = make_person();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002069 "#pod   like($hash->{Name}, qr/^(Mr|Mrs|Miss) \\w+ \\w+$/, \"name ok\");"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002070 "#pod   like($hash->{Phone}, qr/^0[0-9]{6}$/, \"phone ok\");"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002071 "#pod   is(scalar keys %$hash, 2, \"correct number of keys\");"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002072 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002073 "#pod But that's not quite right, what if make_person has a serious problem and"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002074 "#pod didn't even return a hash? We really need to write"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002075 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002076 "#pod   if (ref($hash) eq \"HASH\")"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002077 "#pod   {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002078 "#pod     like($hash->{Name}, qr/^(Mr|Mrs|Miss) \\w+ \\w+$/, \"name ok\");"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002079 "#pod     like($hash->{Phone}, qr/^0[0-9]{6}$/, \"phone ok\");"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002080 "#pod     is(scalar keys %$hash, 2, \"correct number of keys\");"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002081 "#pod   }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002082 "#pod   else"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002083 "#pod   {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002084 "#pod     fail(\"person not a hash\");"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002085 "#pod     fail(\"person not a hash\");"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002086 "#pod     fail(\"person not a hash\"); # need 3 to keep the plan correct"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002087 "#pod   }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002088 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002089 "#pod Already this is getting messy, now imagine another entry in the hash, an"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002090 "#pod array of children's names. This would require"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002091 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002092 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002093 "#pod   if (ref($hash) eq \"HASH\")"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002094 "#pod   {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002095 "#pod     like($hash->{Name}, $name_pat, \"name ok\");"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002096 "#pod     like($hash->{Phone}, '/^0d{6}$/', \"phone ok\");"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002097 "#pod     my $cn = $hash->{ChildNames};"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002098 "#pod     if (ref($cn) eq \"ARRAY\")"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002099 "#pod     {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002100 "#pod       foreach my $child (@$cn)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002101 "#pod       {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002102 "#pod         like($child, $name_pat);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002103 "#pod       }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002104 "#pod     }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002105 "#pod     else"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002106 "#pod     {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002107 "#pod         fail(\"child names not an array\")"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002108 "#pod     }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002109 "#pod   }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002110 "#pod   else"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002111 "#pod   {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002112 "#pod     fail(\"person not a hash\");"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002113 "#pod   }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002114 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002115 "#pod This is a horrible mess and because we don't know in advance how many"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002116 "#pod children's names there will be, we can't make a plan for our test anymore"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002117 "#pod (actually, we could but it would make things even more complicated)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002118 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002119 "#pod Test::Deep to the rescue."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002120 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002121 "#pod =head2 With Test::Deep"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002122 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002123 "#pod   my $name_re = re('^(Mr|Mrs|Miss) \\w+ \\w+$');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002124 "#pod   cmp_deeply("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002125 "#pod     $person,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002126 "#pod     {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002127 "#pod       Name => $name_re,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002128 "#pod       Phone => re('^0d{6}$'),"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002129 "#pod       ChildNames => array_each($name_re)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002130 "#pod     },"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002131 "#pod     \"person ok\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002132 "#pod   );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002133 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002134 "#pod This will do everything that the messy code above does and it will give a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002135 "#pod sensible message telling you exactly what went wrong if it finds a part of"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002136 "#pod $person that doesn't match the pattern. C<re()> and C<array_each()> are"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002137 "#pod special function imported from Test::Deep. They create a marker that tells"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002138 "#pod Test::Deep that something different is happening here. Instead of just doing"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002139 "#pod a simple comparison and checking are two things exactly equal, it should do"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002140 "#pod something else."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002141 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002142 "#pod If a person was asked to check that 2 structures are equal, they could print"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002143 "#pod them both out and compare them line by line. The markers above are similar"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002144 "#pod to writing a note in red pen on one of the printouts telling the person that"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002145 "#pod for this piece of the structure, they should stop doing simple line by line"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002146 "#pod comparison and do something else."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002147 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002148 "#pod C<re($regex)> means that Test::Deep should check that the current piece of"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002149 "#pod data matches the regex in C<$regex>. C<array_each($struct)> means that"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002150 "#pod Test::Deep should expect the current piece of data to be an array and it"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002151 "#pod should check that every element of that array matches C<$struct>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002152 "#pod In this case, every element of C<< $person->{ChildNames} >> should look like a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002153 "#pod name. If say the 3rd one didn't you would get an error message something"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002154 "#pod like"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002155 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002156 "#pod   Using Regexp on $data->{ChildNames}[3]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002157 "#pod      got    : 'Queen John Paul Sartre'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002158 "#pod      expect : /^(Mr|Mrs|Miss) \\w+ \\w+$/"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002159 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002160 "#pod There are lots of other special comparisons available, see"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002161 "#pod L<SPECIAL COMPARISONS PROVIDED> below for the full list."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002162 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002163 "#pod =head2 Reusing structures"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002164 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002165 "#pod Test::Deep is good for reusing test structures so you can do this"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002166 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002167 "#pod   my $name_re = re('^(Mr|Mrs|Miss) \\w+ \\w+$');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002168 "#pod   my $person_cmp = {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002169 "#pod     Name => $name_re,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002170 "#pod     Phone => re('^0d{6}$'),"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002171 "#pod     ChildNames => array_each($name_re)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002172 "#pod   };"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002173 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002174 "#pod   cmp_deeply($person1, $person_cmp, \"person ok\");"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002175 "#pod   cmp_deeply($person2, $person_cmp, \"person ok\");"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002176 "#pod   cmp_deeply($person3, $person_cmp, \"person ok\");"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002177 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002178 "#pod You can even put $person_cmp in a module and let other people use it when"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002179 "#pod they are writing test scripts for modules that use your modules."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002180 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002181 "#pod To make things a little more difficult, lets change the person data"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002182 "#pod structure so that instead of a list of ChildNames, it contains a list of"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002183 "#pod hashes, one for each child. So in fact our person structure will contain"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002184 "#pod other person structures which may contain other person structures and so on."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002185 "#pod This is easy to handle with Test::Deep because Test::Deep structures can"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002186 "#pod include themselves. Simply do"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002187 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002188 "#pod   my $name_re = re('^(Mr|Mrs|Miss) \\w+ \\w+$');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002189 "#pod   my $person_cmp = {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002190 "#pod     Name => $name_re,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002191 "#pod     Phone => re('^0d{6}$'),"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002192 "#pod     # note no mention of Children here"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002193 "#pod   };"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002194 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002195 "#pod   $person_cmp->{Children} = array_each($person_cmp);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002196 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002197 "#pod   cmp_deeply($person, $person_cmp, \"person ok\");"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002198 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002199 "#pod This will now check that $person->{Children} is an array and that every"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002200 "#pod element of that array also matches C<$person_cmp>, this includes checking"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002201 "#pod that its children also match the same pattern and so on."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002202 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002203 "#pod =head2 Circular data structures"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002204 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002205 "#pod A circular data structure is one which loops back on itself, you can make"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002206 "#pod one easily by doing"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002207 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002208 "#pod   my @b;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002209 "#pod   my @a = (1, 2, 3, \\@b);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002210 "#pod   push(@b, \\@a);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002211 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002212 "#pod now C<@a> contains a reference to be C<@b> and C<@b> contains a reference to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002213 "#pod C<@a>. This causes problems if you have a program that wants to look inside"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002214 "#pod C<@a> and keep looking deeper and deeper at every level, it could get caught"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002215 "#pod in an infinite loop looking into C<@a> then C<@b> then C<@a> then C<@b> and"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002216 "#pod so on."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002217 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002218 "#pod Test::Deep avoids this problem so we can extend our example further by"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002219 "#pod saying that a person should also list their parents."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002220 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002221 "#pod   my $name_re = re('^(Mr|Mrs|Miss) \\w+ \\w+$');"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002222 "#pod   my $person_cmp = {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002223 "#pod     Name => $name_re,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002224 "#pod     Phone => re('^0d{6}$'),"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002225 "#pod     # note no mention of Children here"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002226 "#pod   };"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002227 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002228 "#pod   $person_cmp->{Children} = each_array($person_cmp);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002229 "#pod   $person_cmp->{Parents} = each_array($person_cmp);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002230 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002231 "#pod   cmp_deeply($person, $person_cmp, \"person ok\");"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002232 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002233 "#pod So this will check that for each child C<$child> in C<< $person->{Children} >>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002234 "#pod that the C<< $child->{Parents} >> matches C<$person_cmp> however it is smart"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002235 "#pod enough not to get caught in an infinite loop where it keeps bouncing between"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002236 "#pod the same Parent and Child."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002237 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002238 "#pod =head1 TERMINOLOGY"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002239 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002240 "#pod C<cmp_deeply($got, $expected, $name)> takes 3 arguments. C<$got> is the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002241 "#pod structure that you are checking, you must not include any special"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002242 "#pod comparisons in this structure or you will get a fatal error. C<$expected>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002243 "#pod describes what Test::Deep will be looking for in $got. You can put special"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002244 "#pod comparisons in $expected if you want to."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002245 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002246 "#pod As Test::Deep descends through the 2 structures, it compares them one piece"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002247 "#pod at a time, so at any point in the process, Test::Deep is thinking about 2"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002248 "#pod things - the current value from C<$got> and the current value from"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002249 "#pod C<$expected>. In the documentation, I call them C<$got_v> and C<exp_v>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002250 "#pod respectively."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002251 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002252 "#pod =head1 COMPARISON FUNCTIONS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002253 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002254 "#pod =head3 cmp_deeply"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002255 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002256 "#pod   my $ok = cmp_deeply($got, $expected, $name)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002257 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002258 "#pod C<$got> is the result to be checked. C<$expected> is the structure against"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002259 "#pod which C<$got> will be check. C<$name> is the test name."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002260 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002261 "#pod This is the main comparison function, the others are just wrappers around"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002262 "#pod this.  C<$got> and C<$expected> are compared recursively.  Each value in"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002263 "#pod C<$expected> defines what's expected at the corresponding location in C<$got>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002264 "#pod Simple scalars are compared with C<eq>.  References to structures like hashes"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002265 "#pod and arrays are compared recursively."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002266 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002267 "#pod Items in C<$expected>, though, can also represent complex tests that check for"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002268 "#pod numbers in a given range, hashes with at least a certain set of keys, a string"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002269 "#pod matching a regex, or many other things."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002270 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002271 "#pod See L</WHAT ARE SPECIAL COMPARISONS> for details."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002272 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002273 "#pod =head3 cmp_bag"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002274 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002275 "#pod   my $ok = cmp_bag(\\@got, \\@bag, $name)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002276 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002277 "#pod Is shorthand for cmp_deeply(\\@got, bag(@bag), $name)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002278 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002279 "#pod I<n.b.>: Both arguments must be array refs. If they aren't an exception will be"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002280 "#pod thrown."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002281 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002282 "#pod =head3 cmp_set"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002283 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002284 "#pod   my $ok = cmp_set(\\@got, \\@set, $name)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002285 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002286 "#pod Is shorthand for cmp_deeply(\\@got, set(@set), $name)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002287 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002288 "#pod =head3 cmp_methods"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002289 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002290 "#pod   my $ok = cmp_methods(\\@got, \\@methods, $name)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002291 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002292 "#pod Is shorthand for cmp_deeply(\\@got, methods(@methods), $name)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002293 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002294 "#pod =head3 eq_deeply"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002295 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002296 "#pod   my $ok = eq_deeply($got, $expected)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002297 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002298 "#pod This is the same as cmp_deeply() except it just returns true or"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002299 "#pod false. It does not create diagnostics or talk to L<Test::Builder>, but"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002300 "#pod if you want to use it in a non-testing environment then you should"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002301 "#pod import it through L<Test::Deep::NoTest>. For example"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002302 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002303 "#pod   use Test::Deep::NoTest;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002304 "#pod   print \"a equals b\" unless eq_deeply($a, $b);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002305 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002306 "#pod otherwise the L<Test::Builder> framework will be loaded and testing messages"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002307 "#pod will be output when your program ends."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002308 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002309 "#pod =head3 cmp_details"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002310 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002311 "#pod   ($ok, $stack) = cmp_details($got, $expected)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002312 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002313 "#pod This behaves much like eq_deeply, but it additionally allows you to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002314 "#pod produce diagnostics in case of failure by passing the value in C<$stack>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002315 "#pod to C<deep_diag>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002316 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002317 "#pod Do not make assumptions about the structure or content of C<$stack> and"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002318 "#pod do not use it if C<$ok> contains a true value."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002319 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002320 "#pod See L</USING TEST::DEEP WITH TEST::BUILDER> for example uses."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002321 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002322 "#pod =head1 SPECIAL COMPARISONS PROVIDED"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002323 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002324 "#pod In the documentation below, C<$got_v> is used to indicate any given value"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002325 "#pod within the C<$got> structure."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002326 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002327 "#pod =head3 ignore"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002328 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002329 "#pod   cmp_deeply( $got, ignore() );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002330 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002331 "#pod This makes Test::Deep skip tests on C<$got_v>. No matter what value C<$got_v>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002332 "#pod has, Test::Deep will think it's correct. This is useful if some part of the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002333 "#pod structure you are testing is very complicated and already tested elsewhere,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002334 "#pod or if it is unpredictable."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002335 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002336 "#pod   cmp_deeply("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002337 "#pod     $got,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002338 "#pod     {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002339 "#pod       name    => 'John',"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002340 "#pod       random  => ignore(),"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002341 "#pod       address => [ '5 A street', 'a town', 'a country' ],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002342 "#pod     }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002343 "#pod   );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002344 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002345 "#pod is the equivalent of checking"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002346 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002347 "#pod   $got->{name} eq 'John';"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002348 "#pod   exists $got->{random};"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002349 "#pod   cmp_deeply($got->{address}, ['5 A street', 'a town', 'a country']);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002350 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002351 "#pod =head3 methods"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002352 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002353 "#pod   cmp_deeply( $got, methods(%hash) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002354 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002355 "#pod %hash is a hash of method call => expected value pairs."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002356 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002357 "#pod This lets you call methods on an object and check the result of each call."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002358 "#pod The methods will be called in the order supplied. If you want to pass"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002359 "#pod arguments to the method you should wrap the method name and arguments in an"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002360 "#pod array reference."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002361 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002362 "#pod   cmp_deeply("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002363 "#pod     $obj,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002364 "#pod     methods(name => \"John\", [\"favourite\", \"food\"] => \"taco\")"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002365 "#pod   );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002366 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002367 "#pod is roughly the equivalent of checking that"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002368 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002369 "#pod   $obj->name eq \"John\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002370 "#pod   $obj->favourite(\"food\") eq \"taco\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002371 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002372 "#pod The methods will be called in the order you supply them and will be called"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002373 "#pod in scalar context. If you need to test methods called in list context then"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002374 "#pod you should use C<listmethods()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002375 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002376 "#pod B<NOTE> Just as in a normal test script, you need to be careful if the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002377 "#pod methods you call have side effects like changing the object or other objects"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002378 "#pod in the structure. Although the order of the methods is fixed, the order of"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002379 "#pod some other tests is not so if C<$expected> is"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002380 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002381 "#pod   {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002382 "#pod     manager => methods(@manager_methods),"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002383 "#pod     coder => methods(@coder_methods)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002384 "#pod   }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002385 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002386 "#pod there is no way to know which if manager and coder will be tested first. If"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002387 "#pod the methods you are testing depend on and alter global variables or if"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002388 "#pod manager and coder are the same object then you may run into problems."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002389 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002390 "#pod =head3 listmethods"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002391 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002392 "#pod   cmp_deeply( $got, listmethods(%hash) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002393 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002394 "#pod C<%hash> is a hash of pairs mapping method names to expected return values."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002395 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002396 "#pod This is almost identical to methods() except the methods are called in list"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002397 "#pod context instead of scalar context. This means that the expected return"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002398 "#pod values supplied must be in array references."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002399 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002400 "#pod   cmp_deeply("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002401 "#pod     $obj,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002402 "#pod     listmethods("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002403 "#pod       name => [ \"John\" ],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002404 "#pod       [\"favourites\", \"food\"] => [\"Mapo tofu\", \"Gongbao chicken\"]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002405 "#pod     )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002406 "#pod   );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002407 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002408 "#pod is the equivalent of checking that"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002409 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002410 "#pod   cmp_deeply([$obj->name], [\"John\"]);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002411 "#pod   cmp_deeply([$obj->favourites(\"food\")], [\"Mapo tofu\", \"Gongbao chicken\"]);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002412 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002413 "#pod The methods will be called in the order you supply them."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002414 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002415 "#pod B<NOTE> The same caveats apply as for methods()."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002416 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002417 "#pod =head3 shallow"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002418 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002419 "#pod   cmp_deeply( $got, shallow($thing) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002420 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002421 "#pod C<$thing> is a ref."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002422 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002423 "#pod This prevents Test::Deep from looking inside C<$thing>. It allows you to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002424 "#pod check that C<$got_v> and C<$thing> are references to the same variable. So"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002425 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002426 "#pod   my @a = @b = (1, 2, 3);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002427 "#pod   cmp_deeply(\\@a, \\@b);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002428 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002429 "#pod will pass because C<@a> and C<@b> have the same elements however"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002430 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002431 "#pod   cmp_deeply(\\@a, shallow(\\@b))"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002432 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002433 "#pod will fail because although C<\\@a> and C<\\@b> both contain C<1, 2, 3> they are"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002434 "#pod references to different arrays."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002435 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002436 "#pod =head3 noclass"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002437 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002438 "#pod   cmp_deeply( $got, noclass($thing) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002439 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002440 "#pod C<$thing> is a structure to be compared against."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002441 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002442 "#pod This makes Test::Deep ignore the class of objects, so it just looks at the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002443 "#pod data they contain. Class checking will be turned off until Test::Deep is"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002444 "#pod finished comparing C<$got_v> against C<$thing>. Once Test::Deep comes out of"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002445 "#pod C<$thing> it will go back to its previous setting for checking class."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002446 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002447 "#pod This can be useful when you want to check that objects have been"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002448 "#pod constructed correctly but you don't want to write lots of"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002449 "#pod C<bless>es. If C<@people> is an array of Person objects then"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002450 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002451 "#pod   cmp_deeply(\\@people, ["
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002452 "#pod     bless {name => 'John', phone => '555-5555'}, \"Person\","
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002453 "#pod     bless {name => 'Anne', phone => '444-4444'}, \"Person\","
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002454 "#pod   ]);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002455 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002456 "#pod can be replaced with"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002457 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002458 "#pod   cmp_deeply(\\@people, noclass(["
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002459 "#pod     {name => 'John', phone => '555-5555'},"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002460 "#pod     {name => 'Anne', phone => '444-4444'}"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002461 "#pod   ]));"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002462 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002463 "#pod However, this is testing so you should also check that the objects are"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002464 "#pod blessed correctly. You could use a map to bless all those hashes or you"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002465 "#pod could do a second test like"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002466 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002467 "#pod   cmp_deeply(\\@people, array_each(isa(\"Person\"));"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002468 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002469 "#pod =head3 useclass"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002470 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002471 "#pod   cmp_deeply( $got, useclass($thing) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002472 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002473 "#pod This turns back on the class comparison while inside a C<noclass()>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002474 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002475 "#pod   cmp_deeply("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002476 "#pod     $got,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002477 "#pod     noclass("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002478 "#pod       ["
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002479 "#pod         useclass( $object )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002480 "#pod       ]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002481 "#pod     )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002482 "#pod   )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002483 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002484 "#pod In this example the class of the array reference in C<$got> is ignored but"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002485 "#pod the class of C<$object> is checked, as is the class of everything inside"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002486 "#pod C<$object>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002487 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002488 "#pod =head3 re"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002489 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002490 "#pod   cmp_deeply( $got, re($regexp, $capture_data, $flags) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002491 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002492 "#pod C<$regexp> is either a regular expression reference produced with C<qr/.../>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002493 "#pod or a string which will be used to construct a regular expression."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002494 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002495 "#pod C<$capture_data> is optional and is used to check the strings captured by an"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002496 "#pod regex. This should can be an array ref or a Test::Deep comparator that works"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002497 "#pod on array refs."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002498 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002499 "#pod C<$flags> is an optional string which controls whether the regex runs as a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002500 "#pod global match. If C<$flags> is \"g\" then the regex will run as C<m/$regexp/g>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002501 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002502 "#pod Without C<$capture_data>, this simply compares C<$got_v> with the regular"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002503 "#pod expression provided. So"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002504 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002505 "#pod   cmp_deeply($got, [ re(\"ferg\") ])"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002506 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002507 "#pod is the equivalent of"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002508 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002509 "#pod   $got->[0] =~ /ferg/"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002510 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002511 "#pod With C<$capture_data>,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002512 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002513 "#pod   cmp_deeply($got, [re($regex, $capture_data)])"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002514 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002515 "#pod is the equivalent of"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002516 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002517 "#pod   my @data = $got->[0] =~ /$regex/;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002518 "#pod   cmp_deeply(\\@data, $capture_data);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002519 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002520 "#pod So you can do something simple like"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002521 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002522 "#pod   cmp_deeply($got, re(qr/(\\d\\d)(\\w\\w)/, [25, \"ab\" ]))"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002523 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002524 "#pod to check that C<(\\d\\d)> was 25 and C<(\\w\\w)> was \"ab\" but you can also use"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002525 "#pod Test::Deep objects to do more complex testing of the captured values"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002526 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002527 "#pod   cmp_deeply("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002528 "#pod     \"cat=2,dog=67,sheep=3,goat=2,dog=5\","
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002529 "#pod     re("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002530 "#pod       qr/(\\D+)=\\d+,?/,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002531 "#pod       set(qw( cat sheep dog )),"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002532 "#pod       \"g\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002533 "#pod     ),"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002534 "#pod   );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002535 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002536 "#pod here, the regex will match the string and will capture the animal names and"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002537 "#pod check that they match the specified set, in this case it will fail,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002538 "#pod complaining that \"goat\" is not in the set."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002539 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002540 "#pod =head3 all"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002541 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002542 "#pod   cmp_deeply( $got, all(@expecteds) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002543 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002544 "#pod C<@expecteds> is an array of expected structures."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002545 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002546 "#pod This allows you to compare data against multiple expected results and make"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002547 "#pod sure each of them matches."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002548 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002549 "#pod   cmp_deeply($got, all(isa(\"Person\"), methods(name => 'John')))"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002550 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002551 "#pod is equivalent to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002552 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002553 "#pod   $got->isa(\"Person\")"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002554 "#pod   $got->name eq 'John'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002555 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002556 "#pod If either test fails then the whole thing is considered a fail. This is a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002557 "#pod short-circuit test, the testing is stopped after the first failure, although"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002558 "#pod in the future it may complete all tests so that diagnostics can be output"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002559 "#pod for all failures. When reporting failure, the parts are counted from 1."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002560 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002561 "#pod Thanks to the magic of overloading, you can write"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002562 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002563 "#pod   any( re(\"^wi\"), all(isa(\"Person\"), methods(name => 'John')) )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002564 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002565 "#pod as"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002566 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002567 "#pod    re(\"^wi\") | isa(\"Person\") & methods(name => 'John')"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002568 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002569 "#pod Note B<single> C<|> not double, as C<||> cannot be overloaded. This will"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002570 "#pod only work when there is a special comparison involved. If you write"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002571 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002572 "#pod   \"john\" | \"anne\" | \"robert\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002573 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002574 "#pod Perl will turn this into"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002575 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002576 "#pod   \"{onort\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002577 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002578 "#pod which is presumably not what you wanted. This is because perl ors them"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002579 "#pod together as strings before Test::Deep gets a chance to do any overload"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002580 "#pod tricks."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002581 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002582 "#pod =head3 any"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002583 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002584 "#pod   cmp_deeply( $got, any(@expecteds) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002585 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002586 "#pod C<@expecteds> is an array of expected structures."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002587 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002588 "#pod This can be used to compare data against multiple expected results and make"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002589 "#pod sure that at least one of them matches. This is a short-circuit test so if"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002590 "#pod a test passes then none of the tests after that will be attempted."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002591 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002592 "#pod You can also use overloading with C<|> similarly to all()."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002593 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002594 "#pod =head3 Isa"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002595 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002596 "#pod   cmp_deeply( $got, Isa($class) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002597 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002598 "#pod =head3 isa"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002599 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002600 "#pod   cmp_deeply( $got, isa($class) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002601 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002602 "#pod C<$class> is a class name."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002603 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002604 "#pod This uses C<UNIVERSAL::isa()> to check that C<$got_v> is blessed into the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002605 "#pod class C<$class>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002606 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002607 "#pod B<NOTE:> C<Isa()> does exactly as documented here, but C<isa()> is slightly"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002608 "#pod different. If C<isa()> is called with 1 argument it falls through to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002609 "#pod C<Isa()>. If C<isa()> called with 2 arguments, it falls through to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002610 "#pod C<UNIVERSAL::isa>. This is to prevent breakage when you import C<isa()> into"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002611 "#pod a package that is used as a class. Without this, anyone calling"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002612 "#pod C<Class-E<gt>isa($other_class)> would get the wrong answer. This is a hack"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002613 "#pod to patch over the fact that C<isa> is exported by default."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002614 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002615 "#pod =head3 obj_isa"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002616 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002617 "#pod   cmp_deeply( $got, obj_isa($class) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002618 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002619 "#pod This test accepts only objects that are instances of C<$class> or a subclass."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002620 "#pod Unlike the C<Isa> test, this test will never accept class names."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002621 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002622 "#pod =head3 array_each"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002623 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002624 "#pod   cmp_deeply( \\@got, array_each($thing) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002625 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002626 "#pod C<$thing> is a structure to be compared against."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002627 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002628 "#pod <$got_v> must be an array reference. Each element of it will be compared to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002629 "#pod C<$thing>. This is useful when you have an array of similar things, for example"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002630 "#pod objects of a known type and you don't want to have to repeat the same test"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002631 "#pod for each one."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002632 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002633 "#pod   my $common_tests = all("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002634 "#pod      isa(\"MyFile\"),"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002635 "#pod      methods("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002636 "#pod        handle => isa(\"IO::Handle\")"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002637 "#pod        filename => re(\"^/home/ted/tmp\"),"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002638 "#pod     )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002639 "#pod   );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002640 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002641 "#pod   cmp_deeply($got, array_each($common_tests));"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002642 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002643 "#pod is similar to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002644 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002645 "#pod   foreach my $got_v (@$got) {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002646 "#pod     cmp_deeply($got_v, $common_tests)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002647 "#pod   }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002648 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002649 "#pod Except it will not explode if C<$got> is not an array reference. It will"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002650 "#pod check that each of the objects in C<@$got> is a MyFile and that each one"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002651 "#pod gives the correct results for its methods."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002652 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002653 "#pod You could go further, if for example there were 3 files and you knew the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002654 "#pod size of each one you could do this"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002655 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002656 "#pod   cmp_deeply("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002657 "#pod     $got,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002658 "#pod     all("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002659 "#pod       array_each($common_tests),"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002660 "#pod       ["
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002661 "#pod         methods(size => 1000),"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002662 "#pod         methods(size => 200),"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002663 "#pod         methods(size => 20)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002664 "#pod       ]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002665 "#pod     )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002666 "#pod   )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002667 "#pod   cmp_deeply($got, array_each($structure));"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002668 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002669 "#pod =head3 hash_each"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002670 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002671 "#pod   cmp_deeply( \\%got, hash_each($thing) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002672 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002673 "#pod This test behaves like C<array_each> (see above) but tests that each hash"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002674 "#pod value passes its tests."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002675 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002676 "#pod =head3 str"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002677 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002678 "#pod   cmp_deeply( $got, str($string) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002679 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002680 "#pod $string is a string."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002681 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002682 "#pod This will stringify C<$got_v> and compare it to C<$string> using C<eq>, even"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002683 "#pod if C<$got_v> is a ref. It is useful for checking the stringified value of an"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002684 "#pod overloaded reference."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002685 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002686 "#pod =head3 num"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002687 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002688 "#pod   cmp_deeply( $got, num($number, $tolerance) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002689 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002690 "#pod C<$number> is a number."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002691 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002692 "#pod C<$tolerance> is an optional number."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002693 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002694 "#pod This will add 0 to C<$got_v> and check if it's numerically equal to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002695 "#pod C<$number>, even if C<$got_v> is a ref. It is useful for checking the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002696 "#pod numerical value of an overloaded reference. If C<$tolerance> is supplied"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002697 "#pod then this will check that C<$got_v> and C<$exp_v> are less than"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002698 "#pod C<$tolerance> apart. This is useful when comparing floating point numbers as"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002699 "#pod rounding errors can make it hard or impossible for C<$got_v> to be exactly"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002700 "#pod equal to C<$exp_v>. When C<$tolerance> is supplied, the test passes if"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002701 "#pod C<abs($got_v - $exp_v) <= $tolerance>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002702 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002703 "#pod B<Note> in Perl, C<\"12blah\" == 12> because Perl will be smart and convert"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002704 "#pod \"12blah\" into 12. You may not want this. There was a strict mode but that is"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002705 "#pod now gone. A \"looks like a number\" test will replace it soon. Until then you"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002706 "#pod can usually just use the string() comparison to be more strict. This will"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002707 "#pod work fine for almost all situations, however it will not work when <$got_v>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002708 "#pod is an overloaded value who's string and numerical values differ."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002709 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002710 "#pod =head3 bool, true, false"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002711 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002712 "#pod   cmp_deeply( $got, bool($value) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002713 "#pod   cmp_deeply( $got, true );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002714 "#pod   cmp_deeply( $got, false );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002715 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002716 "#pod C<$value> is anything you like but it's probably best to use 0 or 1"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002717 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002718 "#pod This will check that C<$got_v> and C<$value> have the same truth value, that"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002719 "#pod is they will give the same result when used in boolean context, like in an"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002720 "#pod C<if()> statement."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002721 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002722 "#pod B<Note:> C<true> and C<false> are only imported by special request."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002723 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002724 "#pod =head3 code"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002725 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002726 "#pod   cmp_deeply( $got, code(\\&subref) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002727 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002728 "#pod C<\\&subref> is a reference to a subroutine which will be passed a single"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002729 "#pod argument, it then should return a true or false and possibly a string"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002730 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002731 "#pod This will pass C<$got_v> to the subroutine which returns true or false to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002732 "#pod indicate a pass or fail. Fails can be accompanied by a diagnostic string"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002733 "#pod which gives an explanation of why it's a fail."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002734 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002735 "#pod   sub check_name"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002736 "#pod   {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002737 "#pod     my $name = shift;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002738 "#pod     if ($boss->likes($name))"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002739 "#pod     {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002740 "#pod       return 1;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002741 "#pod     }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002742 "#pod     else"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002743 "#pod     {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002744 "#pod       return (0, \"the boss doesn't like your name\");"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002745 "#pod     }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002746 "#pod   }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002747 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002748 "#pod   cmp_deeply(\"Brian\", code(\\&check_name));"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002749 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002750 "#pod =head2 SET COMPARISONS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002751 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002752 "#pod Set comparisons give special semantics to array comparisons:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002753 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002754 "#pod =over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002755 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002756 "#pod =item * The order of items in a set is irrelevant"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002757 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002758 "#pod =item * The presence of duplicate items in a set is ignored."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002759 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002760 "#pod =back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002761 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002762 "#pod As such, in any set comparison, the following arrays are equal:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002763 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002764 "#pod   [ 1, 2 ]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002765 "#pod   [ 1, 1, 2 ]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002766 "#pod   [ 1, 2, 1 ]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002767 "#pod   [ 2, 1, 1 ]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002768 "#pod   [ 1, 1, 2 ]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002769 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002770 "#pod All are interpreted by C<set> semantics as if the set was only specified as:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002771 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002772 "#pod   [ 1, 2 ]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002773 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002774 "#pod All C<set> functions return an object which can have additional items added to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002775 "#pod it:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002776 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002777 "#pod   my $set = set( 1, 2 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002778 "#pod   $set->add(1, 3, 1 );  # Set is now ( 1, 2, 3 )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002779 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002780 "#pod Special care must be taken when using special comparisons within sets. See"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002781 "#pod L</SPECIAL CARE WITH SPECIAL COMPARISONS IN SETS AND BAGS> for details."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002782 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002783 "#pod =head3 set"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002784 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002785 "#pod   cmp_deeply( \\@got, set(@elements) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002786 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002787 "#pod This does a set comparison, that is, it compares two arrays but ignores the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002788 "#pod order of the elements and it ignores duplicate elements, but ensures that all"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002789 "#pod items in C<@elements> will be in C<$got> and all items in C<$got> will be"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002790 "#pod in C<@elements>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002791 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002792 "#pod So the following tests will be passes, and will be equivalent:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002793 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002794 "#pod   cmp_deeply([1, 2, 2, 3], set(3, 2, 1, 1));"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002795 "#pod   cmp_deeply([1, 2, 3],    set(3, 2, 1));"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002796 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002797 "#pod =head3 supersetof"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002798 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002799 "#pod   cmp_deeply( \\@got, supersetof(@elements) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002800 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002801 "#pod This function works much like L<< C<set>|/set >>, and performs a set comparison"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002802 "#pod of C<$got_v> with the elements of C<@elements>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002803 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002804 "#pod C<supersetof> is however slightly relaxed, such that C<$got> may contain things"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002805 "#pod not in C<@elements>, but must at least contain all C<@elements>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002806 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002807 "#pod These two statements are equivalent, and will be passes:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002808 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002809 "#pod   cmp_deeply([1,2,3,3,4,5], supersetof(2,2,3));"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002810 "#pod   cmp_deeply([1,2,3,4,5],   supersetof(2,3));"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002811 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002812 "#pod But these will be failures:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002813 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002814 "#pod   cmp_deeply([1,2,3,4,5],   supersetof(2,3,6)); # 6 not in superset"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002815 "#pod   cmp_deeply([1],           supersetof(1,2));   # 2 not in superset"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002816 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002817 "#pod =head3 subsetof"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002818 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002819 "#pod   cmp_deeply( \\@got, subsetof(@elements) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002820 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002821 "#pod This function works much like L<< C<set>|/set >>, and performs a set comparison"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002822 "#pod of C<$got_v> with the elements of C<@elements>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002823 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002824 "#pod This is the inverse of C<supersetof>, which expects all unique elements found"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002825 "#pod in C<$got_v> must be in C<@elements>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002826 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002827 "#pod   cmp_deeply([1,2,4,5], subsetof(2,3,3)    ) # Fail: 1,4 & 5 extra"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002828 "#pod   cmp_deeply([2,3,3],   subsetof(1,2,4,5)  ) # Fail: 3 extra"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002829 "#pod   cmp_deeply([2,3,3],   subsetof(1,2,4,5,3)) # Pass"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002830 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002831 "#pod =head3 none"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002832 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002833 "#pod   cmp_deeply( $got, none(@elements) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002834 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002835 "#pod @elements is an array of elements, wherein no elements in C<@elements> may"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002836 "#pod be equal to C<$got_v>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002837 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002838 "#pod =head3 noneof"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002839 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002840 "#pod   cmp_deeply( \\@got, noneof(@elements) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002841 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002842 "#pod @elements is an array of elements, wherein no elements in C<@elements> may be"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002843 "#pod found in C<$got_v>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002844 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002845 "#pod For example:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002846 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002847 "#pod   # Got has no 1, no 2, and no 3"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002848 "#pod   cmp_deeply( [1], noneof( 1, 2, 3 ) ); # fail"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002849 "#pod   cmp_deeply( [5], noneof( 1, 2, 3 ) ); # pass"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002850 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002851 "#pod =head2 BAG COMPARISONS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002852 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002853 "#pod Bag comparisons give special semantics to array comparisons, that are similar"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002854 "#pod to L<< set comparisons|/SET COMPARISONS >>, but slightly different."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002855 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002856 "#pod =over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002857 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002858 "#pod =item * The order of items in a bag is irrelevant"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002859 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002860 "#pod =item * The presence of duplicate items in a bag is B<PRESERVED>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002861 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002862 "#pod =back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002863 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002864 "#pod As such, in any bag comparison, the following arrays are equal:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002865 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002866 "#pod   [ 1, 1, 2 ]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002867 "#pod   [ 1, 2, 1 ]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002868 "#pod   [ 2, 1, 1 ]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002869 "#pod   [ 1, 1, 2 ]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002870 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002871 "#pod However, they are B<NOT> equal to any of the following:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002872 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002873 "#pod   [ 1, 2 ]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002874 "#pod   [ 1, 2, 2 ]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002875 "#pod   [ 1, 1, 1, 2 ]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002876 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002877 "#pod All C<bag> functions return an object which can have additional items added to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002878 "#pod it:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002879 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002880 "#pod   my $bag = bag( 1, 2 );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002881 "#pod   $bag->add(1, 3, 1 );  # Bag is now ( 1, 1, 1, 2, 3 )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002882 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002883 "#pod Special care must be taken when using special comparisons within bags. See"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002884 "#pod L</SPECIAL CARE WITH SPECIAL COMPARISONS IN SETS AND BAGS> for details."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002885 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002886 "#pod =head3 bag"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002887 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002888 "#pod   cmp_deeply( \\@got, bag(@elements) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002889 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002890 "#pod This does an order-insensitive bag comparison between C<$got> and"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002891 "#pod C<@elements>, ensuring that:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002892 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002893 "#pod =over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002894 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002895 "#pod =item each item in C<@elements> is found in C<$got>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002896 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002897 "#pod =item the number of times a C<$expected_v> is found in C<@elements> is"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002898 "#pod reflected in C<$got>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002899 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002900 "#pod =item no items are found in C<$got> other than those in C<@elements>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002901 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002902 "#pod =back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002903 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002904 "#pod As such, the following are passes, and are equivalent to each other:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002905 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002906 "#pod   cmp_deeply([1, 2, 2], bag(2, 2, 1))"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002907 "#pod   cmp_deeply([2, 1, 2], bag(2, 2, 1))"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002908 "#pod   cmp_deeply([2, 2, 1], bag(2, 2, 1))"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002909 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002910 "#pod But the following are failures:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002911 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002912 "#pod   cmp_deeply([1, 2, 2],     bag(2, 2, 1, 1)) # Not enough 1's in Got"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002913 "#pod   cmp_deeply([1, 2, 2, 1],  bag(2, 2, 1)   ) # Too many   1's in Got"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002914 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002915 "#pod =head3 superbagof"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002916 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002917 "#pod   cmp_deeply( \\@got, superbagof( @elements ) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002918 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002919 "#pod This function works much like L<< C<bag>|/bag >>, and performs a bag comparison"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002920 "#pod of C<$got_v> with the elements of C<@elements>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002921 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002922 "#pod C<superbagof> is however slightly relaxed, such that C<$got> may contain things"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002923 "#pod not in C<@elements>, but must at least contain all C<@elements>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002924 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002925 "#pod So:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002926 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002927 "#pod   # pass"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002928 "#pod   cmp_deeply( [1, 1, 2], superbagof( 1 )      );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002929 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002930 "#pod   # fail: not enough 1's in superbag"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002931 "#pod   cmp_deeply( [1, 1, 2], superbagof( 1, 1, 1 ));"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002932 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002933 "#pod =head3 subbagof"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002934 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002935 "#pod   cmp_deeply( \\@got, subbagof(@elements) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002936 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002937 "#pod This function works much like L<< C<bag>|/bag >>, and performs a bag comparison"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002938 "#pod of C<$got_v> with the elements of C<@elements>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002939 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002940 "#pod This is the inverse of C<superbagof>, and expects all elements in C<$got> to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002941 "#pod be in C<@elements>, while allowing items to exist in C<@elements> that are not"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002942 "#pod in C<$got>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002943 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002944 "#pod   # pass"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002945 "#pod   cmp_deeply( [1],        subbagof( 1, 1, 2 ) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002946 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002947 "#pod   # fail: too many 1's in subbag"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002948 "#pod   cmp_deeply( [1, 1, 1],  subbagof( 1, 1, 2 ) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002949 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002950 "#pod =head2 HASH COMPARISONS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002951 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002952 "#pod Typically, if you're doing simple hash comparisons,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002953 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002954 "#pod   cmp_deeply( \\%got, \\%expected )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002955 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002956 "#pod is sufficient. C<cmp_deeply> will ensure C<%got> and C<%hash> have identical"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002957 "#pod keys, and each key from either has the same corresponding value."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002958 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002959 "#pod =head3 superhashof"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002960 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002961 "#pod   cmp_deeply( \\%got, superhashof(\\%hash) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002962 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002963 "#pod This will check that the hash C<%$got> is a \"super-hash\" of C<%hash>. That"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002964 "#pod is that all the key and value pairs in C<%hash> appear in C<%$got> but"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002965 "#pod C<%$got> can have extra ones also."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002966 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002967 "#pod For example"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002968 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002969 "#pod   cmp_deeply({a => 1, b => 2}, superhashof({a => 1}))"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002970 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002971 "#pod will pass but"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002972 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002973 "#pod   cmp_deeply({a => 1, b => 2}, superhashof({a => 1, c => 3}))"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002974 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002975 "#pod will fail."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002976 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002977 "#pod =head3 subhashof"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002978 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002979 "#pod   cmp_deeply( \\%got, subhashof(\\%hash) );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002980 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002981 "#pod This will check that the hash C<%$got> is a \"sub-hash\" of C<%hash>. That is"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002982 "#pod that all the key and value pairs in C<%$got> also appear in C<%hash>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002983 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002984 "#pod For example"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002985 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002986 "#pod   cmp_deeply({a => 1}, subhashof({a => 1, b => 2}))"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002987 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002988 "#pod will pass but"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002989 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002990 "#pod   cmp_deeply({a => 1, c => 3}, subhashof({a => 1, b => 2}))"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002991 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002992 "#pod will fail."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002993 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002994 "#pod =head1 DIAGNOSTIC FUNCTIONS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002995 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002996 "#pod =head3 deep_diag"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002997 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002998 "#pod   my $reason = deep_diag($stack);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00002999 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003000 "#pod C<$stack> is a value returned by cmp_details.  Do not call this function"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003001 "#pod if cmp_details returned a true value for C<$ok>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003002 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003003 "#pod C<deep_diag()> returns a human readable string describing how the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003004 "#pod comparison failed."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003005 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003006 "#pod =head1 ANOTHER EXAMPLE"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003007 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003008 "#pod You've written a module to handle people and their film interests. Say you"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003009 "#pod have a function that returns an array of people from a query, each person is"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003010 "#pod a hash with 2 keys: Name and Age and the array is sorted by Name. You can do"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003011 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003012 "#pod   cmp_deeply("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003013 "#pod     $result,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003014 "#pod     ["
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003015 "#pod       {Name => 'Anne', Age => 26},"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003016 "#pod       {Name => \"Bill\", Age => 47}"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003017 "#pod       {Name => 'John', Age => 25},"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003018 "#pod     ]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003019 "#pod   );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003020 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003021 "#pod Soon after, your query function changes and all the results now have an ID"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003022 "#pod field. Now your test is failing again because you left out ID from each of"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003023 "#pod the hashes. The problem is that the IDs are generated by the database and"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003024 "#pod you have no way of knowing what each person's ID is. With Test::Deep you can"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003025 "#pod change your query to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003026 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003027 "#pod   cmp_deeply("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003028 "#pod     $result,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003029 "#pod     ["
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003030 "#pod       {Name => 'John', Age => 25, ID => ignore()},"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003031 "#pod       {Name => 'Anne', Age => 26, ID => ignore()},"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003032 "#pod       {Name => \"Bill\", Age => 47, ID => ignore()}"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003033 "#pod     ]"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003034 "#pod   );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003035 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003036 "#pod But your test still fails. Now, because you're using a database, you no"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003037 "#pod longer know what order the people will appear in. You could add a sort into"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003038 "#pod the database query but that could slow down your application. Instead you"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003039 "#pod can get Test::Deep to ignore the order of the array by doing a bag"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003040 "#pod comparison instead."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003041 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003042 "#pod   cmp_deeply("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003043 "#pod     $result,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003044 "#pod     bag("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003045 "#pod       {Name => 'John', Age => 25, ID => ignore()},"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003046 "#pod       {Name => 'Anne', Age => 26, ID => ignore()},"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003047 "#pod       {Name => \"Bill\", Age => 47, ID => ignore()}"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003048 "#pod     )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003049 "#pod   );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003050 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003051 "#pod Finally person gets even more complicated and includes a new field called"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003052 "#pod Movies, this is a list of movies that the person has seen recently, again"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003053 "#pod these movies could also come back in any order so we need a bag inside our"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003054 "#pod other bag comparison, giving us something like"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003055 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003056 "#pod   cmp_deeply("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003057 "#pod   $result,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003058 "#pod     bag("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003059 "#pod       {Name => 'John', Age => 25, ID => ignore(), Movies => bag(...)},"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003060 "#pod       {Name => 'Anne', Age => 26, ID => ignore(), Movies => bag(...)},"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003061 "#pod       {Name => \"Bill\", Age => 47, ID => ignore(), Movies => bag(...)}"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003062 "#pod     )"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003063 "#pod   );"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003064 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003065 "#pod =head1 USING TEST::DEEP WITH TEST::BUILDER"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003066 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003067 "#pod Combining C<cmp_details> and C<deep_diag> makes it possible to use"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003068 "#pod Test::Deep in your own test classes."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003069 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003070 "#pod In a L<Test::Builder> subclass, create a test method in the following"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003071 "#pod form:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003072 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003073 "#pod   sub behaves_ok {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003074 "#pod     my $self = shift;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003075 "#pod     my $expected = shift;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003076 "#pod     my $test_name = shift;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003077 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003078 "#pod     my $got = do_the_important_work_here();"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003079 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003080 "#pod     my ($ok, $stack) = cmp_details($got, $expected);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003081 "#pod     unless ($Test->ok($ok, $test_name)) {"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003082 "#pod       my $diag = deep_diag($stack);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003083 "#pod       $Test->diag($diag);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003084 "#pod     }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003085 "#pod   }"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003086 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003087 "#pod As the subclass defines a test class, not tests themselves, make sure it"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003088 "#pod uses L<Test::Deep::NoTest>, not C<Test::Deep> itself."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003089 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003090 "#pod =head1 LIMITATIONS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003091 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003092 "#pod Currently any CODE, GLOB or IO refs will be compared using shallow(), which"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003093 "#pod means only their memory addresses are compared."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003094 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003095 "#pod =head1 BUGS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003096 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003097 "#pod There is a bug in set and bag compare to do with competing SCs. It only"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003098 "#pod occurs when you put certain special comparisons inside bag or set"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003099 "#pod comparisons you don't need to worry about it. The full details are in the"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003100 "#pod C<bag()> docs. It will be fixed in an upcoming version."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003101 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003102 "#pod =head1 CAVEATS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003103 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003104 "#pod =head2 SPECIAL CARE WITH SPECIAL COMPARISONS IN SETS AND BAGS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003105 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003106 "#pod If you use certain special comparisons within a bag or set comparison there is"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003107 "#pod a danger that a test will fail when it should have passed. It can only happen"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003108 "#pod if two or more special comparisons in the bag are competing to match elements."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003109 "#pod Consider this comparison"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003110 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003111 "#pod   cmp_deeply(['furry', 'furball'], bag(re(\"^fur\"), re(\"furb\")))"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003112 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003113 "#pod There are two things that could happen, hopefully C<re(\"^fur\")> is paired with"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003114 "#pod \"furry\" and C<re(\"^furb\")> is paired with \"furb\" and everything is fine but it"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003115 "#pod could happen that C<re(\"^fur\")> is paired with \"furball\" and then C<re(\"^furb\")>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003116 "#pod cannot find a match and so the test fails. Examples of other competing"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003117 "#pod comparisons are C<bag(1, 2, 2)> vs C<set(1, 2)> and"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003118 "#pod C<< methods(m1 => \"v1\", m2 => \"v2\") >> vs C<< methods(m1 => \"v1\") >>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003119 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003120 "#pod This problem is could be solved by using a slower and more complicated"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003121 "#pod algorithm for set and bag matching. Something for the future..."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003122 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003123 "#pod =head1 WHAT ARE SPECIAL COMPARISONS?"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003124 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003125 "#pod A special comparison (SC) is simply an object that inherits from"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003126 "#pod Test::Deep::Cmp. Whenever C<$expected_v> is an SC then instead of checking"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003127 "#pod C<$got_v eq $expected_v>, we pass control over to the SC and let it do its"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003128 "#pod thing."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003129 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003130 "#pod Test::Deep exports lots of SC constructors, to make it easy for you to use"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003131 "#pod them in your test scripts. For example is C<re(\"hello\")> is just a handy way"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003132 "#pod of creating a Test::Deep::Regexp object that will match any string containing"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003133 "#pod \"hello\". So"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003134 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003135 "#pod   cmp_deeply([ 'a', 'b', 'hello world'], ['a', 'b', re(\"^hello\")]);"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003136 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003137 "#pod will check C<'a' eq 'a'>, C<'b' eq 'b'> but when it comes to comparing"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003138 "#pod C<'hello world'> and C<re(\"^hello\")> it will see that"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003139 "#pod $expected_v is an SC and so will pass control to the Test::Deep::Regexp class"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003140 "#pod by do something like C<< $expected_v->descend($got_v) >>. The C<descend()>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003141 "#pod method should just return true or false."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003142 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003143 "#pod This gives you enough to write your own SCs but I haven't documented how"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003144 "#pod diagnostics works because it's about to get an overhaul (theoretically)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003145 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003146 "#pod =head1 EXPORTS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003147 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003148 "#pod By default, Test::Deep will export everything in its C<v0> tag, as if you had"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003149 "#pod written:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003150 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003151 "#pod   use Test::Deep ':v0';"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003152 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003153 "#pod Those things are:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003154 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003155 "#pod   all any array array_each arrayelementsonly arraylength arraylengthonly bag"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003156 "#pod   blessed bool cmp_bag cmp_deeply cmp_methods cmp_set code eq_deeply hash"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003157 "#pod   hash_each hashkeys hashkeysonly ignore Isa isa listmethods methods noclass"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003158 "#pod   none noneof num obj_isa re reftype regexpmatches regexponly regexpref"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003159 "#pod   regexprefonly scalarrefonly scalref set shallow str subbagof subhashof"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003160 "#pod   subsetof superbagof superhashof supersetof useclass"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003161 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003162 "#pod A slightly better set of exports is the C<v1> set.  It's all the same things,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003163 "#pod with the exception of C<Isa> and C<blessed>.  If you want to import"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003164 "#pod \"everything\", you probably want to C<< use Test::Deep ':V1'; >>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003165 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003166 "#pod There's another magic export group:  C<:preload>.  If that is specified, all of"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003167 "#pod the Test::Deep plugins will be loaded immediately instead of lazily."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003168 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003169 "#pod =head1 SEE ALSO"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003170 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003171 "#pod L<Test::More>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003172 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003173 "#pod =head1 THANKS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003174 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003175 "#pod Thanks to Michael G Schwern for Test::More's is_deeply function which inspired"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003176 "#pod this library."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003177 "#pod"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00003178 "#pod =cut"
BODY_END
